This study investigates the ground states of a biharmonic Choquard equation, 
$$
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
&\Delta^{2}u+\lambda f(u)=\left(I_{\alpha}(|u|^{p}\right)|u|^{p-2}u, \\
&u\in H^{2}, u>0, u(\infty)=0,
\end{array}
\end{equation}
where the term on the right side features an infinite critical exponent and a confined term, 
$\lambda f(u)$, allowing for various decay rates at infinity for the function $f$. By introducing specific assumptions and performing detailed analysis, we establish the existence of ground states and employ a quantitative deformation argument to demonstrate asymptotic compactness under appropriate conditions, ultimately revealing how ground states behave when $\lambda$ increases without limit.

Within the $U_1$ leptoquark model, we reanalyze the decay $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$, taking into account recent experimental data, which has significantly reduced the allowed parameter space. By incorporating $b 	o c$ decay data, particularly $B( ar{B}^0 	o p 	au  ar{
u})$, we obtain more stringent constraints on the leptoquark mass and coupling strength. Introducing the hadronic form factors from lattice simulations enables precise calculations of relevant decay parameters, although theoretical uncertainties still impact results. At current precision levels, distinct branching ratio values are obtained with and without $b 	o c$ data, but including it shrinks the leptoquark mass allowed by $p 	au$ data from $6.4$ TeV to $4.8$ TeV. Additionally, direct $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ data provides further constraints, particularly on the mass range below $2$ TeV.
